,7,7,7,7,7,7],[7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7],[7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7],[7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7]],"output":[[7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7],[7,7,7,7,4,7,4,7,7,7,7,7,7,7],[7,7,7,7,4,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7],[7,7,7,7,4,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7],[7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7],[7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7],[7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7],[7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7],[7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7],[7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7],[7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,4,4,7,4,4,7],[7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,4,4,7,4,4,7],[7,4,4,7,7,7,7,4,4,4,7,4,4,7],[7,4,4,7,7,7,7,4,4,4,7,4,4,7]]}]},"teaching_variant":"canonical","teacher_models":["claude-opus-5-5"],"explanation":"The grid is a white (7) background with several objects in a single colour. Comparing each input with its output shows that the change is a gravity rule applied object by object.\n\n**Which objects move?** I lined up the object sizes in the fact sheet with what happens to each one:\n- Pair 1: the size-6 object moves and the size-3 object stays.\n- Pair 2: objects of size 9, 6 and 4 move. The size-3 vertical bar and the size-1 dot stay.\n- Pair 3: objects of size 10, 10 and 5 move. The size-3 and size-2 vertical bars stay.\n\nIn every pair, objects with more than 3 cells move and objects with 3 or fewer stay. A shape-based rule (\"only width-1 bars stay\") does not work. The size-3 L in pair 1 is 2 wide and still stays.\n\n**How do they move?** Each moving object keeps its shape and column. It slides straight down until the bottom of its bounding box sits on the last row.\n- Pair 1: the object's bounding box covers rows 1–4 and moves to rows 3–6.\n- Pair 3: the size-5 object covers rows 8–10 and moves to rows 13–15.\n\nNo moving object collides with anything in the examples. So each one can simply be shifted by (last row − bottom of its box).\n\n**Test input:** this is 14×14 with yellow (4) objects of sizes 10, 8, 4, 3 and 1. The objects of size 10, 8 and 4 drop to rows ending at row 13. The size-3 vertical bar and the single dot stay where they are.\n\n**Construction:**\n1. Start from a copy of the input.\n2. For each object larger than 3 cells, erase it and redraw it shifted to the bottom edge. Emit one stage per object moved.\n3. Emit the final grid.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    out = g.copy()\n    H = g.shape[0]\n    objs = objects(g, bg=bg)\n    for o in sorted(objs, key=lambda o: -o[\"size\"]):\n        if o[\"size\"] <= 3:\n            continue\n        y0, x0, y1, x1 = o[\"box\"]\n        dy = H - 1 - y1\n        if dy == 0:\n            continue\n        for (y, x) in o[\"cells\"]:\n            out[y, x] = bg\n        for (y, x) in o[\"cells\"]:\n            out[y + dy, x] = o[\"colour\"]\n        emit(out, f\"Object of size {o['size']} (more than 3 cells) drops {dy} rows so it rests on the bottom edge.\")\n    emit(out, \"Final grid: all objects larger than 3 cells sit on the bottom; small ones stay in place.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"movement":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"It slides straight down until the bottom of its bounding box sits on the last row."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"So each one can simply be shifted by (last row − bottom of its box)."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"For each object larger than 3 cells, erase it and redraw it shifted to the bottom edge."}],"dynamics":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Comparing each input with its output shows that the change is a gravity rule applied object by object."}],"crop":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"It slides straight down until the bottom of its bounding box sits on the last row."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- Pair 1: the object's bounding box covers rows 1–4 and moves to rows 3–6."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"2a2a7d55f6ea597b924157afd2ca297a9b7263ac414d4f33418a23f9d1d956e1","task_sha256":"94cad78be9c547fdff0e200b9058536b87182d541513ff9fd61a371c37f